  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 159 PM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nMoisture plume continues overhead Wednesday night, with rain\nchances continuing well into the night over much of the area.\nForcing for heavy rain continues look strongest over the eastern\nSan Juans, where flash flood watch will continue. Thursday \nlooks like a repeat of Wednesday, as sly mid-level flow brings \nboth moisture and weak s/w energy northward into the area. Flash\nflood watch will continue for the ern San Juans, where models\nsuggest storm totals of over 2 inches by Thu evening. Won't\nissue any further flash flood watches at this point, but will\nhave to closely monitor both the Willow/Aspen Acres scars for\nheavy rainfall. \n\nPacific NW upper trough then moves eastward through the nrn\nRockies Fri/Sat, potentially dragging the main mid-level\nmoisture plume slightly eastward into the eastern mountains and\nplains by Sat afternoon. Still expect another day of widespread\nshowers/storms on Fri, then perhaps a better focus for storms\nover the eastern mountains and plains on Sat, with moist plume\noverhead and weak cold front in the vicinity by Sat evening.\nForecast gets less clear Sun/Mon, as older NBM keeps convective\nthreat going both days, while latest 12z deterministic models\n(GFS/ECMWF) both show increasing wly flow and fairly strong\ndrying over the region both days. Forecast currently reflects\nthe wetter NBM, but potential is growing that we may at least \nsee a decrease in precip chances as we get into early next week.\nTemps Thu/Fri/Sat look mainly seasonal (70s/80s) then slightly \ncooler Sun/Mon as upper trough and cold front swing through.",
